WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
An order is an instruction. An order tells a broker to try to buy or sell. The type you select changes the trade-off between speed and price control.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Market order. A market order seeks prompt execution at available prices. It does not guarantee the price you will receive.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Buy limit order. A buy limit of $50 sets $50 as the most you will pay per share. The order may not execute if a seller is not available at that price.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Sell limit order. A sell limit of $55 sets $55 as the least you will accept per share. The order can remain unfilled.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Stop order. When the stop price is reached, a stop order becomes a market order. The eventual execution price can differ from the stop price.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Stop-limit order. A stop-limit order becomes a limit order once triggered. It adds price control but may never execute.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Review before submitting. Check the action, number of shares, order type, and price fields. Broker choices and order duration may vary.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Compare the trade-offs. Market focuses on execution, limit focuses on a price boundary, and stop adds a trigger. No order type eliminates market risk.
